th  the company  you've managed to calm some concerns  about that I think about their equipment  being used as a backdoor to espionage  but the first of 5g networks in the UK  has already been launched and Huawei  hardware is in it are we looking the  stable door after the horses build it  well we have to make a decision as to  the extent that we use far away we  haven't made that final decision and the  considerations are firstly what you've  just said which is the you know  espionage issue can you be sure that  that equipment isn't being used as a  back door and we have some of the best  technical experts in the world we look  at their advice very very carefully but  the second issue is a strategic one  which is you know China has It's Made in  China 2025 program where they're  explicit that they want to get an 80%  global market share of telecoms  equipment technology and we have to ask  ourselves 5g is going to be something  that dominates our lives  in a way that 3g and 4g never did  because it will control our driverless  cars it will control the heating in our  house and that really a reason why the  more and you've had this debate in  cabinets we know because it kept being  leaked a lot of people just feel  therefore you should just be much firmer  in shutting Huawei out well I'd say this  is not about China this is about whether  we want to become technologically  dependent on any third country when we  know that these technologies are going  to be so important to our future do you  feel that China is an enemy to Western  democracies and to the United Kingdom  was thinks and how did you characterise  China you do a lot about it your wife is  Chinese you've traveled there  extensively what is our relationship  with China absolutely not an enemy we  should welcome the rise of China when  China catches up with the United States  we should remember which some people say  could happen as soon as 10 years time we  should remember their GDP per head will  still on